          "text": "It stops casual editing of the things that were interactive: form fields can no longer be retyped, comments cannot be deleted, and signature fields cannot be cleared. It is not encryption — someone with a PDF editor can still add new content on top, and text that was always static page text remains selectable. For access control, use the encrypt tool as well."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Does flattening remove JavaScript from a PDF?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Yes. Embedded JavaScript actions are stripped along with the interactive form structure. This is a genuine security benefit: PDF JavaScript is a common malware vector, and many corporate mail gateways and government portals reject files that contain it."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Why do portals and printers ask for a flattened PDF?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Because interactive PDFs render inconsistently. A form field can display one value in Acrobat, another in a browser viewer, and print blank from a third application. Flattening collapses everything to fixed page content so what you see is exactly what everyone else sees and what comes out of the printer."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Does flattening reduce file size?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "Usually a little. Removing form definitions, annotation objects, embedded scripts and revision metadata strips real bytes, though the saving is modest because page content dominates. If size is your goal, use the compression tool instead."
        }
      },
      {
        "@type": "Question",
        "name": "Can I flatten a PDF that has a digital signature?",
        "acceptedAnswer": {
          "@type": "Answer",
          "text": "You can, but be careful: flattening alters the document, which invalidates any cryptographic signature applied to it. Flatten before signing, never after."
        }
      },
