      <h2 style="font-size:1.1rem;font-weight:700;color:#fff;margin:28px 0 12px">How to use Flatten PDF</h2>
      <ol style="padding-left:20px;margin:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:10px">
        <li style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;line-height:1.65"><strong style="color:#e4e4e7">Upload your PDF:</strong> Go to ihatepdf.cv/flatten-pdf and upload your document.</li><li style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;line-height:1.65"><strong style="color:#e4e4e7">Review options:</strong> Choose whether to also strip metadata, remove JavaScript, and flatten annotations.</li><li style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;line-height:1.65"><strong style="color:#e4e4e7">Flatten:</strong> Click "Flatten PDF". The process runs locally and takes a few seconds.</li><li style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;line-height:1.65"><strong style="color:#e4e4e7">Download:</strong> Download the permanently flattened, non-editable PDF — no watermark.</li>
      </ol>
      
      <div style="margin-top:28px">
        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;color:#fff;margin:0 0 10px">What flattening actually changes inside the file</h2>
        <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;line-height:1.75;margin:0">A PDF can carry several layers that sit above the printed page: an AcroForm structure defining fillable fields, annotation objects for comments, highlights and stamps, and embedded JavaScript that can run when the file opens. Flattening merges the visible result of all of that into the static page content and then removes the underlying structures. A filled form field stops being a field with a value and becomes ordinary text drawn on the page. A highlight stops being a removable annotation and becomes part of the artwork. The document looks the same and behaves completely differently.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-top:28px">
        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;color:#fff;margin:0 0 10px">Flattening before you send a filled form</h2>
        <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;line-height:1.75;margin:0">This is the case that catches people out. You download a form, fill it in, save it, email it — and the recipient opens it to find empty fields. It happens because some viewers save field values in a way others do not read back, and because a filled field is still editable data rather than committed content. Flattening the PDF before you send it commits your answers to the page, so what you filled in is what every recipient sees, in every application, on every printout.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="margin-top:28px">
        <h2 style="font-size:1.05rem;font-weight:700;color:#fff;margin:0 0 10px">When to flatten a PDF — finalization and compliance</h2>
        <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;line-height:1.75;margin:0">Flattening is the standard final step before distributing any PDF that contains fillable forms, digital signatures, or annotations. Signed contracts should be flattened before archiving to prevent any modification of the signed content. Completed application forms should be flattened before submission to lock in the entered data. Documents containing JavaScript should be flattened before sharing to eliminate any active scripting. Flattening also significantly reduces PDF file size by removing the overhead of interactive elements.</p>
      </div>
      
      <h2 style="font-size:1.1rem;font-weight:700;color:#fff;margin:32px 0 14px">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div style="display:flex;flex-direction:column;gap:18px">
        
        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:0.925rem;font-weight:600;color:#e4e4e7;margin:0 0 5px">How do I make a PDF non-editable?</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;margin:0;line-height:1.65">Flatten it. Upload the PDF at ihatepdf.cv/flatten-pdf and the interactive layers are merged into the static page content, so filled-in form fields become ordinary printed text, annotations become part of the page, and nothing can be clicked, edited or reverted. Download the result — free, with no sign-up and no upload.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:0.925rem;font-weight:600;color:#e4e4e7;margin:0 0 5px">Will flattening stop someone editing my PDF?</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;margin:0;line-height:1.65">It stops casual editing of the things that were interactive: form fields can no longer be retyped, comments cannot be deleted, and signature fields cannot be cleared. It is not encryption — someone with a PDF editor can still add new content on top, and text that was always static page text remains selectable. For access control, use the encrypt tool as well.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:0.925rem;font-weight:600;color:#e4e4e7;margin:0 0 5px">Does flattening remove JavaScript from a PDF?</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;margin:0;line-height:1.65">Yes. Embedded JavaScript actions are stripped along with the interactive form structure. This is a genuine security benefit: PDF JavaScript is a common malware vector, and many corporate mail gateways and government portals reject files that contain it.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:0.925rem;font-weight:600;color:#e4e4e7;margin:0 0 5px">Why do portals and printers ask for a flattened PDF?</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;margin:0;line-height:1.65">Because interactive PDFs render inconsistently. A form field can display one value in Acrobat, another in a browser viewer, and print blank from a third application. Flattening collapses everything to fixed page content so what you see is exactly what everyone else sees and what comes out of the printer.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:0.925rem;font-weight:600;color:#e4e4e7;margin:0 0 5px">Does flattening reduce file size?</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;margin:0;line-height:1.65">Usually a little. Removing form definitions, annotation objects, embedded scripts and revision metadata strips real bytes, though the saving is modest because page content dominates. If size is your goal, use the compression tool instead.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:0.925rem;font-weight:600;color:#e4e4e7;margin:0 0 5px">Can I flatten a PDF that has a digital signature?</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;margin:0;line-height:1.65">You can, but be careful: flattening alters the document, which invalidates any cryptographic signature applied to it. Flatten before signing, never after.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:0.925rem;font-weight:600;color:#e4e4e7;margin:0 0 5px">Does flattening remove hidden metadata?</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;margin:0;line-height:1.65">It removes document-level metadata and revision history carried in the interactive layer. For a thorough clean before sharing — author names, GPS data, tracked changes — run the privacy scanner as well.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:0.925rem;font-weight:600;color:#e4e4e7;margin:0 0 5px">What does flattening a PDF actually do?</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;margin:0;line-height:1.65">Flattening merges all interactive layers — form fields, annotations, and signatures — into the static page content. The visual appearance is preserved exactly, but nothing can be edited, moved, or deleted afterwards.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:0.925rem;font-weight:600;color:#e4e4e7;margin:0 0 5px">Does flattening remove my filled-in form data?</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;margin:0;line-height:1.65">No. The data you entered into form fields is baked into the page visually. It appears exactly as filled — it just can no longer be changed or extracted as field data.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:0.925rem;font-weight:600;color:#e4e4e7;margin:0 0 5px">Is my PDF uploaded to a server?</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;margin:0;line-height:1.65">No. Flattening runs entirely locally using pdf-lib via WebAssembly. Your file never leaves your device.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:0.925rem;font-weight:600;color:#e4e4e7;margin:0 0 5px">What is the difference between flattening and encrypting a PDF?</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;margin:0;line-height:1.65">Flattening makes the content static and non-interactive. Encryption adds a password so the file cannot be opened without the password. For maximum security, you can flatten and then encrypt.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3 style="font-size:0.925rem;font-weight:600;color:#e4e4e7;margin:0 0 5px">Does flattening affect the visual appearance of the PDF?</h3>
          <p style="font-size:0.875rem;color:#a1a1aa;margin:0;line-height:1.65">No. The document looks identical after flattening. Form field values, annotations, and signatures continue to appear exactly where they were — they are just rendered as permanent page content rather than interactive overlays.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      
